WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.314
Idea. Imagine you want to start a car-washing business. You need buckets, sponges and a hose. Where could the money come from? Your savings, selling an old bike, or borrowing from a bank.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:28.164
Finance is the money a business needs to start up, to run day to day, or to grow. Short-term finance is for less than a year, such as paying bills when cash is low. Long-term finance is for big items, such as a new factory.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:38.787
Method. Step one: classify the source. Internal sources come from inside the business or its owner. External sources come from outside.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:50.643
An overdraft lets you take out more than is in your account. Trade credit means buying now and paying the supplier later. A bank loan is a set amount repaid with interest.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:01:06.591
Share capital is money from selling shares, and only companies can do this. Venture capital comes from investors in new, risky businesses, who take a share of ownership. Crowdfunding is many people each giving a small amount online.

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:19.236
Step two: recall each source's features: its cost, how fast it arrives, whether it is repaid, and whether owners lose control. Step three: match the source to the need in the case.

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:34.364
Worked example. Here is a question from the June 2021 assessment materials, Paper one. Explain one benefit to a business of using crowdfunding as a source of finance. It is worth three marks.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:40.516
Mark one: the benefit. A business can raise money even when banks will not lend to it.

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:51.914
Mark two: a new idea with no trading record looks risky to a bank, but many members of the public can each put in a small amount because they like it, so the risk is spread.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:59.283
Mark three: so the business collects the total it needs and its project can go ahead, instead of being dropped.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:10.789
Common mistake. A common mistake is mixing up internal and external. Retained profit and selling assets are internal. A bank loan is external.

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:17.003
Another is saying a sole trader can sell shares. Only companies can raise share capital.

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:33.103
Your turn. Now you try. A bakery needs eight hundred dollars for two weeks to buy flour, until a big wedding order is paid. Which suits it better, an overdraft or share capital? Give one reason. Pause and decide.

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:45.811
Answer. An overdraft. The need is small and short-term. An overdraft is quick to arrange and is soon paid off when the wedding money arrives, and the owners keep full control.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:56.147
That is the pattern for this type: classify the source, match it to the need, then explain its effect on the business. Now try the practice questions.
